1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,744
How do you build something this big in
the middle of the ocean?

2
00:00:04,094 --> 00:00:06,854
There is no road to the construction
site.

3
00:00:07,204 --> 00:00:09,628
A ship moves beneath the crane.

4
00:00:09,978 --> 00:00:15,042
And the finished platform has to face
weather that can stop the entire operation.

5
00:00:15,392 --> 00:00:19,856
The answer starts with the enormous
structure hidden below the waves.

6
00:00:20,756 --> 00:00:22,940
First, a distinction.

7
00:00:23,290 --> 00:00:26,410
Some offshore platforms stand on the
seabed.

8
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,056
Others float.

9
00:00:28,406 --> 00:00:38,102
We are following a fixed steel-jacket platform: a deck supported by a
braced steel framework, with piles securing it into the ground below.

10
00:00:38,452 --> 00:00:43,756
This is one important design, not a
blueprint for every offshore structure.

11
00:00:44,656 --> 00:00:47,080
Much of the work happens on shore.

12
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:54,102
In a fabrication yard, crews assemble the steel framework
and build the equipment modules that will sit above it.

13
00:00:54,452 --> 00:00:59,372
Working here allows large sections to be
put together before they go offshore.

14
00:00:59,722 --> 00:01:04,402
The challenge becomes moving those
sections, then connecting them at sea.

15
00:01:05,302 --> 00:01:10,990
The jacket and deck modules travel offshore
on specialized vessels or barges.

16
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:17,964
Cargo has to be secured for the trip, and the installation
team needs suitable conditions at the destination.

17
00:01:18,314 --> 00:01:25,082
A giant component can be strong enough for service and still
demand extremely careful handling on the way there.

18
00:01:25,982 --> 00:01:30,398
At the site, the jacket has to be placed
upright on the seabed.

19
00:01:30,748 --> 00:01:32,140
Methods vary.

20
00:01:32,490 --> 00:01:38,370
Some jackets are launched and then upended;
others can be lifted directly from a barge.

21
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:43,640
For our illustration, a crane vessel
lifts and positions the jacket.

22
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:47,158
Its movement is controlled throughout
the operation.

23
00:01:48,058 --> 00:01:51,418
Placing the framework is only part of
the job.

24
00:01:51,768 --> 00:01:55,992
Steel piles are driven into the seabed
to secure the foundation.

25
00:01:56,342 --> 00:02:01,166
In this simplified example, they pass
through guides at the jacket legs.

26
00:02:01,516 --> 00:02:09,316
A cutaway makes the connection visible: deck to framework,
framework to piles, piles to the surrounding ground.

27
00:02:10,216 --> 00:02:12,832
Now the upper sections can be installed.

28
00:02:13,182 --> 00:02:20,574
These are called the topsides: the decks carrying equipment
and, on a manned platform, spaces for the crew.

29
00:02:20,924 --> 00:02:25,748
Heavy-lift vessels can place large
modules onto the support structure.

30
00:02:26,098 --> 00:02:32,242
The lift is only one stage; connections, checks
and commissioning still have to follow.

31
00:02:33,142 --> 00:02:38,590
The finished structure has to be designed
for its location and the forces it may face.

32
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:45,180
Its steel frame is part of that response, but
engineering does not make a platform invincible.

33
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:50,210
Inspections, maintenance and weather
planning remain essential.

34
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:55,072
Construction weather and survival
weather are very different problems.

35
00:02:55,972 --> 00:02:59,020
There is also a human side to the
design.

36
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:04,386
A manned platform needs places to work,
rest and gather in an emergency.

37
00:03:04,736 --> 00:03:09,296
The deck is a workplace with routes,
boundaries and limited space.

38
00:03:09,646 --> 00:03:16,894
Equipment and accommodation have to share that space without
turning every journey across it into an obstacle course.

39
00:03:17,794 --> 00:03:24,562
When severe weather threatens, protecting people can mean
shutting in production and evacuating ahead of the storm.

40
00:03:24,912 --> 00:03:30,072
The decisions depend on forecasts, the
facility and its emergency plan.

41
00:03:30,422 --> 00:03:33,590
Escape and rescue arrangements matter
too.

42
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:40,324
A dramatic storm shot should never imply that a
helicopter can simply arrive in any conditions.

43
00:03:41,224 --> 00:03:46,480
In deeper water, the answer may be a
floating system instead of a fixed jacket.

44
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:53,358
A semi-submersible gets support from its buoyant
hull and uses a mooring system to hold position.

45
00:03:53,708 --> 00:03:57,164
It is not standing on legs that reach
the bottom.

46
00:03:57,514 --> 00:04:01,354
Different conditions lead engineers
toward different structures.

47
00:04:02,254 --> 00:04:06,982
So the building above the waves is only
the visible part of the project.

48
00:04:07,332 --> 00:04:17,700
For a fixed jacket platform, fabrication on land, transport at sea, a
piled foundation and carefully planned lifts all have to fit together.

49
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:23,210
What looks like a building in the ocean is a
structure connected to the ground beneath it.
